[FWIW, both LotR and Narnia were childhood favorites of mine, and I'm
enjoying HP from a more adult vantage]

I re-read LWW after seeing Narnia yesterday, and it's the first time I
can remember it taking less time to read the book than to see the
movie.  Indeed, GoF cut many scenes; Narnia actually added a fair
amount pre-wardrobe, much of it unnecessary.

I rate Narnia a cut below GoF (the best of the Potter films to date,
IMO) and 2 cuts below LoTR, but still a good fantasy film.  And it's
certainly superior to LoTR as a children's film, and possibly ahead of
GoF as well.

The Narnia characters are more iconic and less real than those in GoF,
which is maybe good for a kid's story (the morals are more absolutely
drawn, and the character flaws are more cartoonish) but it's a bit
heavy handed for adult viewing, especially when a character instantly
learns a lesson and their character alters immediately and absolutely.
I think this is a deliberate design choice on Lewis' part, making it
more child-accessible--but it does render it less interesting to adult
viewers.

The weak effects in some areas (e.g beavers) were disappointing given
how astonishing some of the other effects were; they were "off" enough
to distract, which neither HP nor LotR have done except for the troll
in Fellowship.

But the effects for Aslan (and others) were top shelf, and Tilda
Swinton did a really brilliant, amazing job as the White Witch.  And
the Lucy was endearing, unlike the BBC's annoying take.



